WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.245 --> 00:00:03.230
Two AI agents can have separate conversations

2
00:00:03.245 --> 00:00:05.250
and still fight over the same browser.

3
00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:06.810
What did I do?

4
00:00:07.245 --> 00:00:08.570
I built a browser pool

5
00:00:08.585 --> 00:00:11.210
to give each identity its own profile

6
00:00:11.225 --> 00:00:13.230
and one owner at a time.

7
00:00:13.925 --> 00:00:16.930
Here, two scripted workers use fictional accounts.

8
00:00:17.205 --> 00:00:19.590
Each has its own Chrome profile and screen.

9
00:00:19.845 --> 00:00:21.350
Both save a different note.

10
00:00:22.205 --> 00:00:22.905
Now watch.

11
00:00:23.485 --> 00:00:25.590
A third worker asks for Studio browser.

12
00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:26.730
It gets BUSY.

13
00:00:26.935 --> 00:00:28.830
Well, the current worker keeps its page.

14
00:00:28.845 --> 00:00:30.830
So what happens next?

15
00:00:31.185 --> 00:00:32.290
After a clean release,

16
00:00:32.305 --> 00:00:34.410
the next worker starts a new session.

17
00:00:34.605 --> 00:00:36.430
The old connection is done.

18
00:00:36.445 --> 00:00:37.370
It stops working.

19
00:00:37.645 --> 00:00:39.070
But the identity and saved note

20
00:00:39.085 --> 00:00:40.190
are all still there.

21
00:00:40.545 --> 00:00:43.190
This is an open source MIT license,

22
00:00:43.205 --> 00:00:45.890
and it runs on Linux or WSL.

23
00:00:46.845 --> 00:00:49.090
So it works with your existing browser driver.

24
00:00:49.185 --> 00:00:51.030
There's also a guide and a source

25
00:00:51.045 --> 00:00:52.250
that are linked below.

26
00:00:52.545 --> 00:00:53.530
Go ahead and give it a try

27
00:00:53.545 --> 00:00:54.250
with two browsers,

28
00:00:54.265 --> 00:00:56.390
and make sure you tell me what you think

29
00:00:56.405 --> 00:00:58.370
and feel free to contribute.

30
00:00:58.385 --> 00:00:59.085
Thank you.
